# Code owners. GitHub requests a review from the owner of every file a pull
# request touches, and the last matching pattern in this file wins.
#
# For a repository with one maintainer this is close to a no-op, and it is here
# anyway for the day it stops being one. The point is not the blanket rule at
# the top; it is the four paths below it, which are the ones where an accepted
# change is difficult to take back.

* @Firelight-Innovations/maintainers

# The tool protocol is the contract the whole open-core model rests on. Every
# tool repository — first-party, commercial, and third-party alike — is written
# against this document, so a change here is a change to somebody else's
# software. It is called out separately from the rest of `docs/` for that
# reason, not because documentation is otherwise unowned.
/docs/tool-protocol.md @Firelight-Innovations/maintainers

# The published surfaces the protocol document describes: the crates other
# repositories depend on, and the npm package a tool author actually installs.
# STANDARDS.md §10 already treats these differently from the application code —
# `missing_docs` and `unreachable_pub` are set here and nowhere else — for the
# same reason they are listed here.
/crates/ @Firelight-Innovations/maintainers
/packages/bridge/ @Firelight-Innovations/maintainers

# The rule book, and the machinery that enforces it. A pull request that relaxes
# a lint, widens a baseline, or edits the gate is a policy change wearing a
# configuration change's clothes.
/STANDARDS.md @Firelight-Innovations/maintainers
/.github/ @Firelight-Innovations/maintainers
